
1. [done] Why we don't account these types of objects:
   The walk preset didn't include unclassified, service, tertiary, pedestrian and steps (e.g. 1000 Steps),
   so they never reached the graph. Added to presets/walk.json (secondary stays avoidance-only).

2. [done] Should these types: steps, service, unclassified and pedestrian be available for walk?
   Yes, plus tertiary. The near-highway penalty is kept.

3. [done] Incorporate starting access point lat and lon in the link of opencellid.org to center on our route starting point.

4. [done] In the plan detail data the "wheather by hours" topic is presented. Use there a pictogram (Sun / Lune) to
   show dark / light time of day.

5. [done] Incorporate starting access point lat and lon in the link of nperf.com to center on our route starting point, don't
   provide a carrier and cell operator, it should be requested by the site.

6. [done] In the plan detail data the "wheather by hours" topic is presented. Use pictograms with a rain (tree levels) or show (tree levels), 
   in the column named "Осадки" (in Russian).
   Done: 💧 (rain) or ❄️ (snow, by weather code) repeated 1-3 times for light / moderate / heavy, before the mm value.

7. [done] Railway stations, metro stations may be used for crossing long line obects like railways, highload roads and so on.
   However, additionaly looking for details of implementation a given station on the Internet is a good idea.
   Done: route_graph.add_station_crossings links the two sides of a railway / motorway-trunk-primary road at each
   station or halt (always on); the SKILL asks for a web search on every station the route crosses at.

8. [done] Add to README.md the one short and comprehensive example how to ask skills to create a route, build a map and
   plan a day hike, it should be complete enough and be a good example for users. Add it right after "## What it does",
   to be visible from the document's beginning.


9. [done] Change which highway types the cyclist presets (`bike-leisure` and `bike-sport`) and the walk preset can use.
   Background: docs/research/2026-10-11-highway-types-and-access.md.
   Done (0.3.0): bike presets got the new types; `fallback_highway` (service, road) and `bicycle_explicit_highway`
   (footway, pedestrian) edges cost x1000; steps cost x5 with a note; Overpass regex anchored (no `*_link`).
   a) Cyclists (sport and leisure): permit `tertiary`, `unclassified`, `service` and `road`. Use `service` in full
      (all `service=*` subtypes), but only as a fallback to restore connectivity: the router uses a `service` way
      only when the graph has no other link. Do the same for `road` if it is also a fallback (decide while implementing).
   b) Cyclists (sport and leisure): permit `steps`. Remove `steps` from `hard_exclude_tags` in the bike presets.
      (A cyclist pushes the bicycle on steps: add a cost penalty and a note in the route notes.)
   c) Cyclists (sport and leisure): permit `corridor` (a passage inside a building).
   d) Cyclists (sport and leisure): `footway` and `pedestrian` need an explicit `bicycle=yes` or `bicycle=designated`.
      Without it, use them only as a fallback to restore connectivity.
   e) Pedestrians and cyclists (sport and leisure): permit `bridleway`.
   Also: add a test for each rule, check the unanchored Overpass regex for `*_link` values (see the research
   document), and update the SKILL files, README and spec for the changed presets.

10. [done] Enforce a minimum route length. Today the distance budget is only an upper limit (`select_optional_points`
    adds points under the limit and nothing adds length). A route of 0.16 km was saved for a 30 km request and no
    check stopped it. Source: feedback/01_osm-test.
    Done (0.3.0): presets.resolve_length_range, waypoints.check_minimum_length / check_enough_points,
    archive_validate minimum check, SKILL steps 6, 10, 15.
    a) The user can give both a minimum and a maximum length (or time). Then use these two values.
       If the user gives only one length (or time), it is the maximum, and the minimum is 1/2 of it. The planner sets
       this default minimum by itself and does not ask the user for it. The planner must not save a route shorter
       than the minimum.
    b) If the user gives neither a length nor a time, ask the user for one of them.
    c) If the range [minimum length, maximum length] gives no route, do not save a route. Tell the user why and ask
       what to change (the range, the start point, the area radius or the mode).
    d) If there is not much information to choose points of interest, ask the user (for example: what kind of places
       the user likes, or which places the user knows).
    e) If the points of interest that the planner found are not enough, ask the user. Do not fill the route silently
       with a route that has only the start point or only a shop.
    Also: add a check in `archive_validate` (the route is shorter than the minimum -> error), add tests, and update
    the SKILL files and the spec.

11. [done] If a route is shorter than the minimum length and the planner cannot lengthen it through more points of interest,
    do not add length with empty spurs or trips to an arbitrary point without the permission of the user (source: the
    route makarovskoe-vdkhr-bike-2026-10-11, where a 1.26 km dead-end spur to a random node was added to reach the
    minimum, and notes.md called it a spur to a picnic site). Tell the user and offer these options:
    a) reduce the minimum length;
    b) give new information, so that the planner can review the points of interest;
    c) try another order of the chosen points of interest to get a longer route;
    d) allow the planner to add arbitrary segments that do not lead to any point of interest.
    The planner uses option d) only after the user chooses it, and then says in notes.md that the length was added
    by such segments (not as a visit to a place). Also: a route with a large share of repeated segments (for
    example more than 30 %) must not be called a loop without saying how much is repeated; add a check and tests,
    and update the SKILL files and the spec.
    Done (0.4.0): waypoints.SHORT_ROUTE_OPTIONS (in RouteTooShortError), route_graph.repeated_share,
    waypoints.repeat_statement, weights padding_allowed / padding_km, archive_validate checks, SKILL step 15, spec 3.5.1.

12. [done] Trunk and primary roads without cycle infrastructure are excluded for cyclists by default, and the planner
    gave no way to use them (source: road 65К-4105000, where the user's override was lost at a rebuild of the weights).
    Do not allow them by default. When points are unreachable without such a road, probe with it, tell the user the
    road and the length, and ask. Use the road only after the user agrees, as a last-resort (fallback) way.
    Done (0.5.0): presets.allow_main_roads, route_graph.main_road_length_km, SKILL (bike presets rules), spec 3.5.2,
    tests; overrides are kept at a revision (revise_weights).

13. [done] Protected zones (nature reserves, protected areas) are closed always. Military zones stay closed always.
    For the other protected zones: if a point of interest cannot be reached, or the route cannot be built, without
    crossing one, probe, tell the user the zone and the length, and ask. Cross it only after the user agrees.
    Done (0.6.0): route_graph.is_protected_only / split_protected / protected_zones_crossed,
    presets.allow_protected_zones, SKILL step 8, spec 3.5.3, tests.

14. [done] In the detail plan view, the weather table should contain the "..." hyperlink in the Wind column after the wind data.
   It should point to windy.com to see the situation in the location for a given hour on the planned date.
   The zoom should be taken to keep all your route in the view. Here are details:
   "Yes, you can generate a direct web link that opens the Windy.com map at specific coordinates, with a selected weather
    layer and for a specific date/time. No API is required for this. The link structure is as follows:
    https://www.windy.com/?[DATE-TIME],[LATITUDE],[LONGITUDE],[ZOOM]
    Use this code with caution."
   Done (0.7.0): weather.windy_link / windy_zoom, link in the Wind column (time in UTC rounded to the 3-hour grid Windy shows, checked in Chrome; zoom fits the route bbox), tests.
